Z||000011||filthyscarecrow||03-14-2001||12:33 PM||||i remeber running 1/10th scale oval first with an RC10L (the wide version) then everyone went to the superspeedway version and we couldn't afford the new kit so my dad made one out of spare aluminum lying around the house. it was a bit heavier, but he managed to make it work adn i won a couple A mains. then i tried roadcourse and never went back. we tried some of the bigger oval events too, but i had tweaked the aerodynamics (cut fender holes in the body like the GTP cars had behind the front wheels- won with it the first time out) and when that body got destroyed, used a spare that didn't have the aero tweaks and couldn't get the car to stick. oh well. that was on the largest oval track in the country at the time. but like i sed, i never really liked oval as much as roadcourse. i did a regional F-1 race and qualified like 7th for the final using my friend's car that he had just boiught the weekend before. mine would have cleaned up, but all the moda i made were illegal. i kind of miss those days. now you pretty much have to have your own dyno and lap top to even be competitive, much less win. back then, clean driving would take you all the way. i'm hanging on to my stuff though in case i ever have kids....<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000012||LZeitgeist||03-14-2001||01:04 PM||||Cheever - <P>Choreographed swordfighting and hand-to-hand types of combat, done as part of a performance at either science-fiction/fantasy conventions, or outdoor performances at Renaissance Faires...<P>Formula88 up there has been known to swing a pretty mean claymore (at my head, usually) on occasion, too... he's a member of the same group.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The choreography is based on learning the actual combat techniques with the different weapons chosen to use and adapting those real-life techniques into safe, repeatable moves that appear realistic, but are controlled and safe to perform with a partner.<P>I prefer recreating swordfights from movies and TV (Highlander (movies and TV series); Star Wars; Gladiator; Robin Hood; etc.), but the group also does a large number of original pieces with choreography created from scratch.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pontiaddict||03-14-2001||04:56 PM||||I got hooked when a friend of mine bought a Traxxas T-maxx. I ended up getting a Stampede, broke it. Got a Associated RC10B3 broke that too. (spend the extra money to get the factory team version if you ever buy one, the Sport version sucks) Now I'm looking at a Tamiya touring car that the same friend got off of ebay. maybe I'll fix the stampede one of these days. It looked cool with the custom 50 Chevy sedan delivery body on it (Used to be Grave Digger body) although it would look better on the touring car body if if the track width wasn't too wide to make it fit.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||03-14-2001||07:55 PM||||I have SOOOOOO much R/C stuff! We used to really be into that stuff big. We would go all over the south east to race. My favorite place is SliverWings in College Park Ga. Indoor concrete oval. We always raced 12L's, but my friends raced the 1/10's. Before quiting, we were racing 1/12 scale with 4 cell modifieds. It was much cheaper than the big cars (tires and parts) and I was winding my own custom motors- brother in law has a computer operated motor dyno for these things. One of my motors cranked almost 100K rpms, but had no low end power. We could not gear the car down enough to make it pull. But it was hell in the parking lots! Oh the memories. . . BTW- Met the lady that is now my wife while at the track. So the moral of the story: Try to be nice to the new kid at the race track, he probably has a good looking older sister!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
